# GSAP Animation Rules

## Plugin Registration

- Register GSAP plugins once at the top of files: `gsap.registerPlugin(Flip, ScrollTrigger)`
- Don't register plugins inside components or hooks

## Flip Animations

- Use `opacity, transform` props for smooth button/layout transitions
- Always capture state before DOM changes with `requestAnimationFrame`
- Keep `absolute: false` for normal flow elements
- Clean up flip state refs after animation

## Performance Optimization

- Use `useGSAP` hook for automatic cleanup
- Minimize props captured - only capture what you need
- Use early returns to avoid unnecessary calculations
- Combine state capture and DOM updates in single frame

## Code Patterns

### Button Flip Animation

```typescript
const handleAction = () => {
  if (!containerRef.current) return;

  flipStateRef.current = Flip.getState(
    containerRef.current.querySelectorAll('.target-elements'),
    { props: 'opacity, transform' }
  );

  requestAnimationFrame(() => {
    setStateChange(true);
    onAction();
  });
};

useGSAP(() => {
  if (!stateChange || !flipStateRef.current) return;

  Flip.from(flipStateRef.current, {
    duration: 1,
    ease: 'power2.inOut',
  });

  flipStateRef.current = null;
}, [stateChange]);
```

### ScrollTrigger Animations

```typescript
useGSAP(() => {
  gsap.to(elementRef.current, {
    scrollTrigger: {
      trigger: elementRef.current,
      start: 'top 80%',
      toggleActions: 'play none none reverse',
    },
    // animation properties
  });
});
```

## Best Practices

- Keep animation logic in custom hooks when reusable
- Use TypeScript refs: `useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null)`
- Minimize DOM queries - cache selectors when possible
- Use semantic class names for animation targets
